{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the son of an Israelite woman went out.</b> The meaning is: he went out from his house, or from his dwelling place." "<b>In the midst of the children of Israel.</b> That he came from his place into the midst of the people. <b>And they strove in the camp.</b> And it is possible to explain that the language of 'went out' — without specifying from where — is like the expression (1 Kings 22:21) 'ויצא הרוח' ('and a spirit came forth'), and in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 8:10) 'ובכן ראיתי רשעים קבורים ובאו' ('and so I saw the wicked buried, and they came'), and this is what is written (Job 1:21) 'ערום יצאתי מבטן אמי וערום אשוב שמה' ('naked I came out of my mother's womb, and naked I shall return there')." "<b>And it appears</b> to me that to this our Sages, blessed be memory, alluded in their Midrash, where they said: 'ויצא בן אשה ישראלית' — Rabbi Levi said: from his world he went out. And therefore the passage of 'ויצא' was juxtaposed to the word 'עולם.' And the meaning of 'from his world he went out' is that the human being is a microcosm (עולם קטן), and this is what is written (Ezekiel 36:24) 'ומארצי יצאו' ('and they shall go out from their land'), and it is written (Ezekiel 15:4) 'מהאש יצאו' ('from the fire they came out') — the fire of flesh and blood, and this is clear." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'ויצא בן אשה ישראלית' — from where did he go out? From the passage above he went out, to mock, and said: 'on the day of the Sabbath, on the day of the Sabbath they shall arrange it' (Leviticus 24:8) — the way of a king to eat hot bread every day, perhaps cold bread of nine days? The Mishnah said: from the court of Moses he went out condemned. He came to pitch his tent within the camp of Dan. They said to him: what is your business here? He said to them: I am of the daughters of Dan. They said to him: 'איש על דגלו באתת לבית אבתם' — 'each man by his standard, with signs, to the house of their fathers' (Numbers 2:2), and not to the house of their mothers. He entered the court of Moses and went out condemned. He arose and blasphemed." "<b>And he was the son of an Egyptian man.</b> Whom Moses killed, who had been a taskmaster and overseer over the husband of Shelomit. <b>In the midst of the children of Israel</b> — this teaches that he converted." "<b>And they strove in the camp.</b> One should ask: if the two of them were quarreling with each other, why did he curse the Name over this? It would have been fitting for him to rage against Moses, or to strike and kill him. But it is possible to say that the Israelite man told him the matter of his father who was killed, and mentioned to him how his death came about — for Moses killed him using the Explicit Name — and therefore 'the son of the Israelite woman blasphemed the Name and cursed,' that is, the Name that he had heard at Sinai he spelled out and cursed. And therefore the section of the blasphemer was juxtaposed to the section of 'at Mount Sinai'; and because he did not wish to come to the Tent of Meeting, therefore 'they brought him to Moses,' and for this reason it does not say 'to Moses and to all the congregation' as is said regarding the gatherer (of wood)." "<b>And I</b> ask here: why did the Torah need to recount this incident that happened to them in the wilderness? It would have been fitting to conceal the matter for the honor of HaShem and not to reveal it at all, but merely to inform Israel of the law in general terms and say, 'Any man who curses his Elohim shall bear his sin,' and 'he who pronounces the Name of HaShem shall be put to death' — for even the children of Noah are warned against cursing the Name, and needless to say Israel. And it appears proper to say that the intention of the Torah in recounting this incident is for two benefits: the first, to inform us that no one from Israel stumbles in so grievous a sin except the consummate wicked transgressor who is fundamentally flawed in his origin from the side of his father and mother; and the second, that from here a great principle and an enormous corner in the matter of blessings will be clarified for us, and how a person is obligated to bless HaShem — for the blasphemer first spelled out HaShem in its letters and afterward cursed it; this is what it says, 'the son of the Israelite woman blasphemed HaShem and cursed,' and he was liable to the punishment of death for this. And correspondingly, in the matter of blessing and reward, when one blesses HaShem he must first reflect in his heart upon the spelling of the letters of HaShem and what they signify, and concentrate on them in his thought, and afterward bless HaShem and mention it with his mouth, and with this he will receive his reward in full and live eternal life. A mnemonic for this is what is written (Psalms 145): ״ארוממך אלהי המלך ואברכה שמך לעולם ועד״ — that is, 'I shall exalt You' first in my thought and in the intention of the heart, and afterward 'and I shall bless'; in the manner of what they said: 'A person should always enter two cubits [of prayer] and afterward pray.'" "<b>Son of the Israelite woman.</b> This comes to inform us that the child of a gentile who lies with an Israelite woman is not an Israelite. And even though we ruled in the Talmud that in the case of a gentile who lies with an Israelite woman the child is valid — whether she is unmarried or a married woman — yet they said 'they defile the offspring,' meaning that he is blemished for the priesthood, and all the more so he is not an Israelite by name for the purpose of genealogy in the standards and in the inheritance of the land, for it is written of them (Numbers 26), 'to the names of the tribes of their fathers.' Thus wrote the Ramban, z״l."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:23.066223194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}